{start_of_verse: Verse 6}
[Dm]Ad we talked of the other new worlds we'd discover as [Em7b5]she gave up her body to me,
[A7]As I chopped up her mainsail for timber, I told her of [Dm]all that we still had to see.
[D]As the frost turned her moorings to nine-tails and the [Gm]wind lashed her sides in the cold,
I [C]burned her to keep me alive every night in the [F]loving embrace of her [A7]hold.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 7}
I won’t call it rescue, [Dm]that brought me back here to this old world [Em7b5]to drink and decline,
And [A7]pretend that the search for another new world was [Dm]well worth the burning of mine.
But [D]sometimes at night, in my dreams, comes the singing of [Gm]some unknown tropical bird,
And I [C]smile in my sleep, thinking Annabel Lee's finally [F]made it to another new [A7]world.
Yeah, [D]sometimes at night in my dreams comes the singing of some [Gm]unknown tropical bird
And I [C]smile in my sleep, thinking Annabel Lee's finally [F]made it to another [A7]new world
{end_of_verse}

